you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's program  electric car company tesla hits the  brakes on cryptocurrency  bitcoin because of its climate impact  how farmers could help the environment  and make agriculture  more sustainable  and we meet sammy safari the  award-winning conservationist  saving sea turtles in kenya  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions we aim to take you to the  heart of a climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come now they're one of the biggest  names in the car industry  and last year produced the world's  best-selling electric  vehicle and now tesla says it's not  going to accept  bitcoin as a form of payment according  to the company's chief executive  elon musk it's because of the impact the  cryptocurrency has  on the climate anisha sethi reports  [Applause]  tesla the brainchild of elon musk is a  name synonymous with electric cars  and clean energy in march this year  the company announced it would accept  bitcoin as a form of payments  now a u-turn in a bid to make transport  more sustainable  the ceo has suspended the decision  in a tweet musk said we are concerned  about rapidly increasing use of fossil  fuels for bitcoin  mining and transactions especially coal  which has the worst emissions of any  fuel  cryptocurrency is a good idea on many  levels and we believe it has a promising  future  but this cannot come at great cost to  the environment  analysts see the move as an attempt to  assess concerns about sustainability  there may be some sort of connection to  the change in political winds in the us  but i think this is quite possibly just  about an ongoing debate inside tesla  between a wing of the business that is  totally connected to these this is the  silicon valley mindset  and the other side of the business  that's perhaps more connected to more  responsible shareholders and the more  environmentally friendly focus of the  business  selling electric cars as a replacement  for combustion engines  bitcoin enthusiasts insist mining mainly  involves renewable energy  however it can be an intense activity  that often relies on electricity  generated with fossil fuels  although tesla doesn't plan to sell any  of its bitcoin investment  it hopes to use it for transactions if  mining shifts to more sustainable energy  in future  anisha sethi sky news  well isabel asher hamilton is senior  reporter at insider and joins me now  so how can a virtual currency be energy  intensive  well um it comes down to how these  um bitcoins are mined uh which is done  mostly using hardware so when someone  wants to  mine a bitcoin they have to basically  get  a computer or lots of computers to solve  lots and lots of calculations which is  very energy intensive  but if it's renewable energy that's used  presumably that's  not so much of a problem absolutely if  it was all renewable energy that was  being used  to power these computers that would be  fine but there's lots of  i mean it's studies kind of vary on how  much of the energy being used already is  renewable  but there's quite a few that say that  loads of the energy  is coming from like elon musk in his  tweet  coal and things like that so yes  theoretically if it was all  renewable electricity it would be fine  but that's not the reality  and when you have a big figure like elon  musk making  a statement like this what does that  mean for the future of bitcoin and also  tesla  it's true that elon musk is a bit of a  folk hero almost among sort of  crypto currency enthusiasts and there  was a reaction  um the value of bitcoin did sink a  little bit  when he made this announcement tesla is  a huge company to accept  uh a currency that only recently started  becoming even a tiny bit mainstream  but you know bitcoin has is already  worth so much more than it used to be  worth  and like he said he's not getting rid of  the investment yet so it's really hard  to predict  isabel asha hamilton thank you in  today's other climate news  new research by energy company bulb has  found households in the north of england  are being put off  buying greener vehicles that's because  there are far fewer  charging points compared with the south  bulb wants the government to invest in  more  public charging points to make  low-carbon cars more affordable and  accessible for everyone  microplastic pollution found in rivers  across the uk  is caused by water suppliers data from  researchers at manchester university  suggests companies are releasing  wastewater into slow-moving rivers which  are unable to break up the pieces of  plastic  experts believe microplastics can alter  entire ecosystems  and harm wildlife  [Applause]  john kerry will travel to europe for  climate talks ahead of the cop 26  conference  the white house has confirmed the u.s  climate envoy will visit the uk  italy and germany during his trip which  starts on thursday  it comes after the united states raised  its carbon reduction targets to as much  as  52 percent by 2030  [Applause]  hundreds of people have been given the  opportunity to visit wetlands to improve  their mental health  the wildfowl and wetlands trust and the  mental health foundation  have launched the blue prescribing  scheme which uses nature as a therapy  for anxiety and depression  the six-week course will give people the  chance to stay near wetlands  and take part in activities such as bird  watching and pond dipping  and people on the isle of wight are  making a feathery new resident feel at  home  chief a rare white-tailed sea eagle has  been brought from scotland to live at  robin hill nature park  as part of a reintroduction program  farmers need more help to secure their  livelihoods and help them tackle  climate change and nature loss according  to a new report from the institute for  public policy research  agriculture is responsible for around 10  percent of the uk's greenhouse gas  emissions  but that figure is decreasing and you  can see here the estimated greenhouse  gas emissions for agriculture in the uk  between 1990 and 2018 and emissions for  carbon dioxide  methane and nitrous oxide are all lower  in 2018  that's the blue bars than they were in  1990 which in yellow  and if we look at the total greenhouse  gas emissions for the sector  they've fallen by nearly nine million  tons in that period  but much more needs to be done if the uk  is to reach net zero emissions by  2050. well fraser hugo is a farm  farmer based in north yorkshire and he  joins me now welcome to you  just how important an issue is this for  you are there steps that you've been  able to  to take to ensure that your farm is more  environmentally friendly  absolutely i know it's we're looking you  know part of our ethos is looking at  what we can do for nature  and and by definition when we're doing  things for naturals it's doing things  for the climate but but sort of  importantly we are a business as well  so what we're trying to do is is also  make our  business not more financially  sustainable  moving forward well yes and the  government says that it's got  ambitious and far-reaching sets of  investments they say  they've got plans that are designed to  incentivize sustainable farming  practices but  do you think the more that could be done  what kind of help could you do with  well you know we we have a lot of talk  about it and i suppose  the key is going to be seeing the action  over the next sort of two to three years  as we go through this  transition as we sort of leave behind  the european system of  farm support that we've had since 19th  the 1970s  and move into a payment for public goods  support system  um that is making all the right noises  to help us do that  but like i say it's it's going to be the  action on the ground in terms of  both the both the financial but also the  sort of cultural  change that hopefully we can eat in bed  and do  do you agree that farmers could do more  there's no point sort of  um denying that um we  you know we work within a certain  environment i think we we live with that  we  we live with climate change more than  anybody else you know where  my business is um is impacted by the  weather and ultimately the climate  um every every year it's very  stereotyping but that's  probably one of the main things we talk  about so but yeah there is there is more  that we can do  wait we're on a journey um i think we're  at the start of what could be quite an  exciting journey in terms of  the measures to to tackle both climate  change and  and nature recovery i think when we talk  about climate change we've really got to  talk about this recovery as well  because there's a huge task ahead in  terms of that  fraser hugo good to talk to you thank  you  thank you the watamu marine national  park on  kenya's coast is home to more than 600  species of fish  and seven species of turtle but  increasing tourism  high unemployment and a surge in illegal  fishing  are putting them at risk now one man's  efforts to help communities manage the  ecosystem more sustainably  has been rewarded with a prestigious  international prize  we'll hear from sami safari shortly but  first here's why he's saving  africa's sea turtles  five of the seven different species of  sea turtle  can be found along the kenyan coast  under threat from bycatch and poachers  who illegally sell them for meat and oil  sammy has worked tirelessly to convince  fishers to stop  taking turtles turning  poachers into protectors  and sami safari joins me now from the  watamu marine  national park summit safari huge  congratulations to you  tell us more about how your project  works  so uh so i am sami safari i work for  local austrian conservation in guatemala  kenya that's the kenyan coast  i lead an anti-poaching team and i  am also in the community development  team  so in general i do work so closely with  the fishers community  and in the futures community i do  education and awareness  talking to members of the local  community the importance of  nature and everything  so and the whole marine ecosystem  why is it so important to you to do this  work and to protect the marine ecosystem  it's really good for me to to do all  this work because  it hurts us have a resource  that will be  beneficial for the present and the  future generation  sami how are you going to spend your  winnings with the weekly fund  this is mainly to support  conservationists  working so closely with  local communities from the grassroots  to benefit wildlife and inhabitants  and even people so  with my funding or the word  i've already uh i got from a weekly  which i'm  really really thankful i'll use this one  to  improve governancy i'll also use it  to increase conservation and  biodiversity  reduce illegal poaching of both  fauna and the floor last but not least  financial management and monitoring  in community groups sami safari thanks  very much indeed  and that's everything from us for today  coming up tomorrow how  cornwall could hold the key to the uk's  green energy future thanks for watching  see you then

